  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA cold front will continue to slowly pass overhead, stalling\nover the coastal waters this evening. Lifting low pressure to\nthe west will track towards the region, advancing this front\nnorthward again Thursday as a warm front.\n\nClouds will tend to fill in tonight, with mostly overcast skies\ncome Thursday morning. The thickening clouds will tend to help\ninsulate temps tonight, with lows remaining in the mid 50s to\naround 60 despite calm winds. Dry conditions prevail through\nThursday morning, but shower chances increase across western NH \ninto mid- afternoon. Compact low will begin to strengthen as is\npasses through central New England and southern NH into southern\nME. Overlapping with a brief deep moisture profile, an area of\nrain should develop and expand as it nears coastal ME. This\nshould be fairly progressive, and only a narrow corridor may see\nbeneficial rainfall vs. lighter more showery conditions outside.\nBecause of the antecedent dry conditions, there is little to no\nthreat of flooding.\n\nRain should be exiting the coastal communities after midnight\nand before daybreak, with improving conditions into the day\nFriday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn upper low will move into the Gulf of Saint Lawrence from \nQuebec during the day on Friday as a surface cold front moves \noff into the Atlantic. High pressure will start to nudge in \nbehind the front, tightening the pressure gradient while \nushering in strong cold air advection and steep low level lapse \nrates. These factors all point to quite a windy day on Friday \nwith forecast soundings suggesting the potential for some gusts \nin the 30 to 35 mph range out of the northwest. A few spotty \nshowers and/or lingering clouds may remain in place over the \nmountains but most will see dry weather plenty of sunshine. \nWinds should diminish rather quickly as we lose daytime heating \nand the pressure gradient starts to relax a bit. \n\nSaturday will see lighter winds as the center of high pressure \nmoves into Quebec. This pattern will also lead to plenty of \nsunshine and below normal temperatures with highs mainly in the \nlow to mid 60s.\n\nThe next shortwave and cold front will start to approach on \nSunday which will lead to our next chances (40 to 70 percent) \nfor rain. While there is still plenty of uncertainty regarding \ntiming and the eventual track, models have started come into \nbetter agreement that southern New Hampshire and southwest Maine\nwill see the best chances. The NBM currently suggests that \nthese areas have a 40 to 50 percent chance of a half inch of \nrain or greater while probabilities decrease moving north and \neast. Most of the rain will move out Sunday night and we should \nremain dry until the next front approaches sometime mid to late \nweek. Temperatures should remain below normal well into next \nweek with highs mainly in the 60s.",
